## Execution order

### Gate 0 — Freeze and prove the inventory

Run the verifier before and after every extraction:

```bash
node scripts/internal/verify-runtime-kernel-trust-audit.mjs
```

It fails on a changed baseline count, missing/extra/duplicate/reordered allowlist method, duplicate audit ID, either retired method resurfacing, an unregistered inherent implementation, or namespace-ledger drift.

Coverage policy is fail-closed: the verifier recursively scans every Rust
source file below `crates/services/src` for inherent implementations and aliases
of `RuntimeKernelService`, `RuntimeOwnerServices`, `RuntimeProjectionService`,
and `RuntimeEffectCompatibilityGateway`. Each type may have inherent methods
only in its registered owning source file. The verifier inventories every
visibility-qualified associated callable, including `pub(...)` restricted
visibility and `const`/`async`/`unsafe`/`extern` modifier combinations. Private
helpers and trait-implementation methods are outside these public inventories.
An unrecognized impl header, public callable form, or top-level macro invocation
inside an inherent impl fails verification rather than assuming it cannot add a
callable.

### Gate 1 — Trace consequential call paths

For every `unresolved` crossing:

1. enumerate all Rust callers and every mounted route, protocol adapter, MCP/native bridge, compatibility shim, and test helper;
2. identify the owner-required primitive for that method: domain writer/admission authorization, participant admission, linkage to a prior authorized effect, or an external-effect `AuthorityGrant`/`CapabilityLease`;
3. for accepted truth or evidence, prove the owner/participant admission and exact linkage to the admitted invocation and prior effect authority where applicable;
4. only for an external capability effect, identify where issuer/signature, holder, audience, scope, target, budget, expiry, revocation epoch, idempotency key, and proof-of-possession are verified;
5. identify the final writer or invoker and the exact transition/effect material committed into its receipt;
6. fail the extraction if any caller reaches accepted truth outside its owner gate or reaches an external effect outside the applicable authority PEP.

An owner/participant ref, writer flag, lease-shaped ref, authority-ref list,
caller-supplied boolean, or receipt-ref list is evidence to trace, not proof of
owner authorization, admitted reachability, or effect-authority linkage.

### Gate 2 — Extract owner mechanics without splitting the gate

Move one owner family at a time. Non-effect planners and owner admissions now live on `RuntimeOwnerServices`; compatibility no longer preserves them on the trusted type. The eight effectful candidates remain on `RuntimeEffectCompatibilityGateway` until their full pre-effect authority, zero-effect-on-denial, and post-effect receipt paths are proven. Owner code may return proposals or effect observations; consequential results still re-enter the final invariant-bearing owner admission and receipt/truth handoff.

### Gate 3 — Separate projections

Move read-only projections behind owner-qualified read services. Enforce compile-time dependency direction where practical: projections may consume immutable accepted records and projection checkpoints, but cannot import writers, authority issuers, effect invokers, or receipt appenders.

### Gate 4 — Resolve compatibility debt

The two retirement candidates are absent from every service surface. Keep the verifier denial permanent: the lease-decision constructor must never return as a synthetic lease issuer or verifier, and any future lease use must consume an Authority-provider-issued, daemon-verified chain.

### Gate 5 — Re-run adversarial proof

At minimum, each moved family needs:

- bypass-route and alternate-adapter denial;
- missing, forged, expired, revoked, wrong-holder, wrong-audience, wrong-scope, and replayed authority denial when authority is required;
- target, body, model/provider, artifact/result, policy, predecessor/head, and receipt swaps;
- duplicate-idempotency and retry-double-effect refusal;
- same-hash/foreign-signer and foreign-domain refusal where signed truth is involved;
- crash/restart proof that no partial accepted truth or orphan effect is reported as complete;
- parity proof that legitimate outputs remain stable across the move.

## Completion condition

The service/type-boundary decomposition is complete when the verifier and focused compile/negative proofs pass: `RuntimeKernelService` then contains only its constructor and 35 invariant-bearing methods. Physical kernel-namespace decomposition is **not** complete. It closes only when every entry in the residual ledger is safely split or moved, all `unresolved` consequential paths converge on their owner-qualified admission/writer gate and applicable effect-authority PEP, every extraction has the listed negative proof, and the remaining `runtime/kernel/` modules contain only invariant-bearing admission, authority verification, receipt/evidence binding, accepted-truth ordering/persistence, replay, and stable invocation-contract mechanics.
